[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

How would you like to approach this? Feel free to take a moment to gather your thoughts first.

[candidate · 1:30] Let me lay out structure. Profit = revenue − costs. Revenue grew 15%, so the decline is almost certainly cost-side, but I'll verify revenue quality first.

Three branches:

One, revenue quality. Is the 15% growth from new store openings versus same-store sales? If they opened stores, revenue grows while fixed costs and ramp-up losses grow faster. I'd also want menu-price history — have prices moved at all in two years?

Two, costs. Split fixed versus variable. Variable: coffee beans, milk, cups, hourly labor. Fixed: rent, salaried staff, corporate overhead, marketing, any new capex or debt service.

Three, mix. Shift from high-margin drinks toward low-margin food, or more discounting and loyalty redemptions.

Do we have a cost breakdown as a percent of revenue for both years, and a split of store count then versus now?
