[candidate · 3:02] Costs, specifically the variable side. Flat store count kills the expansion hypothesis, so revenue growth is same-store — either price or volume. And if revenue is up 15% while margin fell from 24% to 6%, costs grew much faster than revenue.

Quick math: index revenue to 100 two years ago, profit 24, costs 76. Today revenue 115, profit 6.9, costs 108.1. So costs rose 32.1 on a base of 76 — about 42% growth against 15% revenue growth. That's a huge gap, and it's too big to be overhead creep on a flat footprint. That points to COGS or hourly labor, the lines that scale with volume, plus possibly a rent step-up at lease renewal.

So: decompose that 108, find which line grew most, and check whether prices were ever raised to pass through input inflation.
