[interviewer · ANALYSIS · 3:05] COGS is 58% of revenue today, up from 42% two years ago. Labor is 22% of revenue, and it’s been stable. Overhead is 14% of revenue, up slightly from 12%. Menu prices have not changed in two years. Those are the cost lines and the price history you asked for. Which line explains most of the margin decline, and how did you work that out?

[candidate · 4:35] COGS explains essentially all of it. Margin fell 18 points; COGS rose 16 points of revenue, overhead 2, labor 0. So COGS is ~89% of the decline.

And since prices haven't moved in two years, the 15% revenue growth is pure volume — they're selling more units at flat prices while input costs per unit have risen. Coffee and dairy have run up hard, and they've absorbed 100% of it.

Recommendation: raise prices and fix input cost. Take a targeted price increase of roughly 10–12% — on a flat cost base that recovers most of the 16 points, and specialty coffee demand is fairly inelastic, especially if phased and weighted toward add-ons like milk alternatives and food. In parallel, lock in bean and dairy supply with forward contracts or renegotiated vendor terms, and audit waste and portioning at store level.

Risks: volume loss if competitors hold price, and commodity prices reverting so contracts lock you high. Next steps: elasticity test in two or three markets, and a line-item breakdown of COGS to separate beans, dairy, and packaging.

I'm done — can we do another case?
